A||||4||PaUL||Balljoint Update
Z||000000||PaUL||04-15-99||01:38 PM||||Hi all,<p>Just an update on my earlier posts concerning balljoints. After having a lwr/fr joint coming apart, I was always afraid the other side would do the same if I drove to hard. I ordered new ones for the front and just got them this morning. I ordered them through the net. Looking at the top balljoints, I see that they have slotted bolt holes and look just like the adjustable ones from the fiero store. These are standard Moog replacements. Hmm I guess FS is just hyping their product. <p>Ok, well now I have to install them, maybe someone can tell me the best way to get the old balljoints out? should I use a picklefork? or is there some other tool I can use.<p>
Z||000001||Patrick||04-15-99||02:26 PM||||Hi Paul,<p>After reading your posts, I thought I'd check the balljoints on my '87 Fiero. It appears that one of the front lower balljoints is shot.<p>According to my Haynes manual, the whole lower control arm needs to be replaced. Seems like a poor design to me. Anyway, I suppose something like this is best to buy new, or would it be worthwhile to go to the wreckers and get a used lower control arm with of course, the balljoint attached?<p>Any comments?<br>            <br> <br>
Z||000002||PaUL||04-15-99||03:58 PM||||Patrick,<br>I have the same Haynes manuel, that info must be outdated. When I popped one of my front lower balljoints, I replaced just the joint. Had to take the lower A arm in to have the balljoint pressed out and new one pressed in.<p>If I were you, Id change that balljoint asap. The fear of having a balljoint separate at high speed is always at the back of my mind. <p>I also would like to apologize for my past remarks to Motorhead in my post marked "Bad Balls, Bad Experience" It just sounded like he was saying that if I kill myself in my Fiero, its my own damn fault. The more I thought about, the more I knew he was right, thats why Im changing all the fronts.
Z||000003||Motorhead||04-15-99||04:43 PM||||No apology needed Paul! Sorry if I came across the wrong way.<p>In 1982 my '67 Sunbeam dropped a rod end while I was showing off to a buddy with a gorgious Porsche 911. Allways gave him that old line about how Porsches allways leave the road in ONE direction, BACKWARDS! <p>Sure enough, when the rod end went, "we left the road backwards".<p>This old Sunbeam had a great British Racing Green paint job, and original knock-off wire wheels!<p>We cleaned out a ditch, and wiped out a couple rows of a local farmers peanut crop.<p>All total, I ended up replacing two wheels, a rear bumper,one tail light lens,and paying a body shop to remove a small dent in a rear fender, the paint never matched again on the left rear quarter.<p>My 19 year old son now asks to borrow the Fiero occasionally,he knows better than to ask about the Z-car, and I hand over the keys reluctantly with the classic "old fart Dad" line. "You scratch it and it's your butt!"<p>Sound familiar anyone.<p>Anyway, thanks Paul! Your insight,and tolerance, is greatly appreciated. 
Z||000004||theogre||04-15-99||07:42 PM||||Fiero store, focoa, and some other fiero "specialty" places are good for unique items only. All of them place a major markup on nearly all the "standard" parts. Always check your regular pep boys, strauss, autozone, type place first. And call around. Prices vary widely between even large retailers. and sometimes that little hole in the wall down the street beats them all.
